<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anacaona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1474–1503</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anacaona was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> noblewoman and ruler of Jaragua in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caonabo">Caonabo</a> of Maguana and sister of the cacique <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohechio">Bohechío</a>, whom she succeeded. Celebrated as a poet and composer of areítos, the Taíno songs and dances, she received the Spanish with hospitality. In 1503 Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-ovando">Nicolás de Ovando</a> had her chiefs burnt at a feast at Jaragua and Anacaona hanged.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anacaona.jpg" alt="Anacaona" width="313" height="504" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vida y viajes de Cristóbal Colón / por Washington Irving. Madrid: Impr. de Gaspar y Roig, 1852 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vida_y_viajes_de_Crist%C3%B3bal_Col%C3%B3n-1852-Honores_Tributados_%C3%A0_la_Reina_Anacaona_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
